    "stem": "Tejas colours a $6$-by-$6$ grid in checkerboard black and white, then removes two opposite corner squares that are the same colour. He wants to cover the remaining region with $2 \\times 1$ tiles that do not overlap and leave no gaps.\n\nWhich claim is correct?",
    "item_type": "mcq",
    "options": {
      "A": "The answer is the word checkerboard, because that colouring word is printed with the grid.",
      "B": "Yes: the remaining region has $34$ squares, an even count, so $2 \\times 1$ tiles can cover it.",
      "C": "No: each $2 \\times 1$ tile covers one black and one white square, but the remaining region has unequal black and white counts.",
      "D": "The same colouring argument also settles tiling of every even-area shape, including shapes with no grid colouring."
    },
